WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:02:33.790 --> 00:03:57.120
HAASS: Well, welcome everybody to the Council
on Foreign Relations.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:03.890
And it is evening here in New York, but guess
what it is, John?

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.340
It is morning in South Africa.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.340
(Laughter.)

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:12.549
I've been waiting all day to say that.

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:16.090
But it is morning in South Africa.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.959
This is John Campbell.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:21.390
He is the author of this book.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:28.400
He is also the Ralph Bunche senior fellow
for Africa policy studies here at the Council

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.659
on Foreign Relations.

00:04:30.659 --> 00:04:31.960
It's not his first book.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:38.590
Several years ago he put out a book about
Nigeria called "Nigeria: Dancing on the Brink,"

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:39.800
which it continues to do.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.210
CAMPBELL: Yes, it does.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:49.349
HAASS: And he writes a blog that appears regularly
on CFR.org, and also edits the Nigeria security

00:04:49.349 --> 00:04:50.349
tracker.

00:04:50.349 --> 00:05:01.430
John's a former ambassador, including Nigeria,
and he held many jobs at the State Department.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.650
What he and I are going to do is chat for
a few minutes, then we are going to open it

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.900
up to you all to ask the difficult questions.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.900
And then afterwards, you have two obligations,
responsibilities.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:22.340
One is to eat and drink a little bit and enjoy
yourself, but two is to buy a copy of "Morning

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:27.090
in South Africa," because if you don't he'll
have to change the title and have to add the

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.000
letter U to the first word.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:40.979
So work with me here, work with me here.

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:46.180
As the title suggests, this book is upbeat.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:52.699
And that, in some ways, is the area of where
I think the rubber meets the road or whatever

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:55.710
other clich� one wants to turn to.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:01.699
It's now just over two decades since the dismantling
of apartheid.

00:06:01.699 --> 00:06:08.360
So before we talk about where we are and where
we might be heading and what justified, you

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:13.620
know, the optimism of John's title, let's
talk about the last 22 years.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.800
I mean, we now have the advantage of hindsight.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:17.800
CAMPBELL: We do.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:25.580
HAASS: First, if you will, first two post-apartheid
decades, closing in on a quarter century.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.729
How do you think historians and, more importantly,
how do you think of it?

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:36.020
What do you think-what should we take as the
last two decades if you were going to rate

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.020
it, if you will?

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.010
And what did happen as opposed to what could
have happened, how things have gone, the trajectory?

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:45.000
What's your take on it all?

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:55.930
CAMPBELL: Legally, constitutionally, institution-building
and politically, the past two decades have

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:59.909
been remarkably successful.

00:06:59.909 --> 00:07:10.560
In terms of addressing poverty, bringing about
fundamental social change and the economic

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:19.910
development of the roughly half the population
that's mired in poverty, progress has been

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:26.760
painfully slow, and indeed even disappointing.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:37.870
I see certain parallels between what has happened
in South Africa and what has happened in my

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.600
native American South.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:50.240
And that is, it is much easier to address
some of the political and legal issues than

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:58.950
it is the social and economic consequences
of some three centuries of white supremacy.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:03.839
HAASS: I didn't know you well two-plus decades
ago.

00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:09.110
I'm curious, have things turned out better,
worse or pretty much what you anticipated?

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:23.120
CAMPBELL: Better in that there is a vibrant,
dynamic democracy in which human rights are

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:32.349
protected to a greater extent probably than
anywhere else in the world.

00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:43.280
But probably for most South Africans, there
has been remarkably little change in the way

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.680
they actually live.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:53.920
This, by the way, includes the rich as well
as the poor.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:09:02.680
All institutions in South Africa, from universities
to country clubs, are now integrated.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:13.209
Segregation has been dismantled, and yet segregation
persists.

00:09:13.209 --> 00:09:24.490
Interesting polling data that shows that straight
across racial lines most people have relatively

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:32.270
little interaction with people of the other
races in South Africa.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:42.420
And of those who have the most, they tend
to be white people, white people because of

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:50.880
their need for service personnel who, of course,
are predominantly black or colored.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:57.650
But for most people, change in their personal
circumstance has not been particularly dramatic.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:04.930
HAASS: And for those blacks and others in
South Africa who have done well financially,

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:09.650
obviously a minority, but who have done well
financially, is it different between people

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.550
of, if you will, a certain class?

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.100
Has class broken down racial barriers?

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:20.370
CAMPBELL: Many South Africans think and hope
that it has, and certainly within the black

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:27.019
community there is greater class differentiation
now than there was 20 years ago.

00:10:27.019 --> 00:10:33.620
There is a small number of black oligarchs
who attract a good deal of media attention,

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.570
very small in number.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:46.589
There is a larger black middle class basically
coming out of the public sector, civil servants,

00:10:46.589 --> 00:10:51.639
employees of state-owned enterprises and so
forth.

00:10:51.639 --> 00:10:58.709
However, I think it's sort of important to
remember that even under apartheid there was

00:10:58.709 --> 00:11:07.240
a small, black middle class and there were
even a few very rich black South Africans.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:12.519
Still, the black middle class is larger now
than it was then and considerably more influential.

00:11:12.519 --> 00:11:17.019
HAASS: Why is South Africa, for whatever problems
it's run into, and we're going to talk about

00:11:17.019 --> 00:11:22.420
some of those in a minute, why has it done
so much better than Zimbabwe?

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:30.550
CAMPBELL: Two, maybe three fundamental reasons.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:35.990
Let's start with the easiest, the quality
of leadership.

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:41.040
Just compare Robert Mugabe with Nelson Mandela.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.290
And Nelson Mandela was not all by himself.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:51.569
Nelson Mandela was surrounded by a cohort
of leaders of extremely high quality, reminiscent

00:11:51.569 --> 00:12:00.680
perhaps in some ways of the first Washington
administration here.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.930
Start with that.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:11.470
Move on to what I think is of central importance,
and that is the rule of law and constitutionalism,

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:16.899
rule of law deeply ingrained in South Africa.

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:21.540
One can argue that the rule of law, to a greater
or lesser extent, has existed there since

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:26.670
the 17th century albeit applying to only a
very small proportion of the population.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:31.550
Still, it did not have to be invented out
of whole cloth.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.540
In Zimbabwe, vastly more recent.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:39.760
British administration was established only
in the 1890s.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:47.819
There was a very small settler class, maybe
230,000 out of a population of between 6 (million)

00:12:47.819 --> 00:12:53.189
and 7 million, which behaved in a particularly
exploitative way.

00:12:53.189 --> 00:13:03.160
Further, the settler class was remarkably
rigid and unwilling to accommodate.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:09.120
One of the really central things about South
Africa is they're very good at doing deals.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:15.820
In the aftermath of the Boer War, essentially
the Boers and the British did a deal.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:21.980
Essentially, political power went to the Boers,
but the British were able to retain a domination

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:24.089
of the economy.

00:13:24.089 --> 00:13:33.850
At the time of the transition in 1993, '94,
'94, essentially a deal was done whereby political

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:42.819
power moved from the small, white minority
to political parties controlled by the black

00:13:42.819 --> 00:13:48.970
majority, but property remained in white hands.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:52.399
HAASS: Is that why you haven't had enormous
white flight from South Africa?

00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:53.399
CAMPBELL: That's true.

00:13:53.399 --> 00:13:54.399
And you haven't had.

00:13:54.399 --> 00:13:59.590
Actually, the exact numbers are that there
are slightly more white people in South Africa

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:03.660
now than there were in 1994.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:09.019
Another bumper sticker that illustrates the
same point, I think, is that the gulf between

00:14:09.019 --> 00:14:17.620
white wealth, not income, wealth and everybody
else in South Africa is greater now than it

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:18.720
was in 1994.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.350
In other words, whites have gotten richer
with the dismantling of apartheid.

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:29.439
HAASS: You've spoken to some of the good things,
but it's hard to look at some of Mr. Mbeki's

00:14:29.439 --> 00:14:33.399
leadership and most of Mr. Zuma's and feel
good about it.

00:14:33.399 --> 00:14:42.129
So why has this happened, because it can't
just be because of the characteristic of these

00:14:42.129 --> 00:14:43.129
individuals?

00:14:43.129 --> 00:14:49.230
People, yes, people obviously matter, leaders
matter, but also the times have to allow or

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:50.550
enable it.

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:51.559
So what does that tell us?

00:14:51.559 --> 00:14:57.379
Why shouldn't we be more worried than you
seem to be or to basically say that the future

00:14:57.379 --> 00:15:02.690
of South Africa, if it looks more like Zuma
than it does like Mandela, it's not going

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:04.730
to be a happy South Africa?

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.110
So help me understand this.

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:13.119
CAMPBELL: And there are plenty of South Africans
who are deeply worried about the trajectory

00:15:13.119 --> 00:15:17.380
of the Zuma administration.

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.309
How can this be?

00:15:20.309 --> 00:15:28.329
First of all, Zuma, and Mbeki towards the
end of his time in office, increasingly surrounded

00:15:28.329 --> 00:15:33.980
by a smaller and smaller sort of coterie of
people.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:43.230
And in the case of Zuma, I would suggest that
many of them are at least slightly paranoid.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:48.149
There was a secretary general of the African
National Congress who a couple of months ago

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:54.759
accused the American embassy in Pretoria of
plotting regime change through the use of

00:15:54.759 --> 00:15:56.230
exchange programs.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:16:07.319
It's a kind of atmosphere of paranoia that,
who knows, may remind us of other politicians.

00:16:07.319 --> 00:16:19.389
What has happened is that the institutions
that are in place have limited what the Zuma

00:16:19.389 --> 00:16:23.869
administration can actually do.

00:16:23.869 --> 00:16:27.519
There's a kind of pattern that we see.

00:16:27.519 --> 00:16:38.480
The latest episode, the latest example-the
administration has proposed legislation that

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:51.809
would limit funding that NGOs can receive
from abroad, and also impose on them pretty

00:16:51.809 --> 00:16:54.629
onerous reporting requirements.

00:16:54.629 --> 00:16:59.149
HAASS: Different than NGOs which are not receiving
money from abroad?

00:16:59.149 --> 00:17:01.029
CAMPBELL: Exactly.

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:07.439
Now, the pattern is the legislation is tabled.

00:17:07.439 --> 00:17:14.040
The opposition parties, who are extremely
skilled, they know every parliamentary trick

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:25.820
in the book, normally force massive revision
of such legislation.

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:33.380
When the revised legislation is finally passed,
it's then challenged in the courts, and the

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:38.880
courts either throw it all out or they throw
out bits and pieces of it.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.990
It's a fairly regular kind of process.

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:47.500
We saw it late last year with the so-called
secrecy bill which would have greatly reduced

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:54.770
the ability of whistleblowers, for example,
to operate in public space.

00:17:54.770 --> 00:18:01.780
HAASS: So let's talk now for a minute about
the present and the future.

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.860
Mr. Zuma has how much longer to lead?

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:06.650
CAMPBELL: Well, that's a very interesting
question.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:17.870
He could be out as early as September if the
ANC does badly in the upcoming local elections.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:22.860
If he is not booted out by the leadership
of the party, which is increasingly discontented

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.850
with him, then he could stay on for another
year.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:31.150
HAASS: OK, either way then, but no longer
than a year and a half?

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:32.150
CAMPBELL: No, that's right.

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:33.150
HAASS: OK.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:34.530
So what is your sense of who or what comes
afterwards?

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:45.981
CAMPBELL: The most-discussed successors to
Jacob Zuma are Cyril Ramaphosa, a millionaire

00:18:45.981 --> 00:18:56.549
businessman who helped negotiate the transition
to nonracial democracy and who is unfortunately

00:18:56.549 --> 00:19:03.690
much more popular in London and New York than
he is in South Africa.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:16.140
A second is Madam Zuma, that's Jacob Zuma's
ex-wife, now head of the AU Commission, former

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:25.720
minister of health during Mbeki's more disreputable
HIV/AIDS stage, and also as foreign minister

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:30.650
a person quite prepared to look the other
way with respect to Zimbabwe.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.750
She is the candidate of Jacob Zuma.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:41.840
She is also the candidate of the ANC Women's
League, a very powerful body.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:48.150
A third candidate is the current speaker of
the national assembly, interestingly also

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:55.590
a woman, so two of the three most-discussed
candidates to be the next president of South

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:56.590
Africa are women.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.560
That's relatively unusual in Africa.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:08.410
My own feeling is that the next leader of
the party, the next chief of state will be

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:16.980
thrown up by the political system and will
probably be a person that most of us don't

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:19.500
talk about very much and don't know very well.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:20.760
HAASS: Do you think it may be none of the
above?

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:24.990
CAMPBELL: I think it almost certainly will
be none of the above.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.460
HAASS: OK, so if I don't remember the names
it's all right.

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:28.790
CAMPBELL: That's right.

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:30.120
HAASS: That's good.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:31.120
(Laughter.)

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:36.010
And your sense is that more important that
the person is the institutionalization and

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.530
the political culture.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:45.530
Again, consistent with your upbeat title,
your sense is that Zuma is more likely to

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:49.500
be, I don't know if the word is aberration,
but something of a Nader rather than necessarily

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:56.890
the precedent or the new normal because of,
again, culture, political culture, the strength

00:20:56.890 --> 00:20:59.470
of the rule of law, the strength of the media,
and so forth?

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:00.950
CAMPBELL: That's correct.

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:05.270
Let me give you two examples.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:14.270
South Africa is the only country in Africa
that permits gay marriage.

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:21.060
South Africa has also abolished capital punishment.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:28.770
In both cases, this was the result of judicial
decisions based on human rights provisions

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:32.110
of the constitution.

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:38.760
Both are extremely unpopular, and they are
particularly unpopular amongst the constituents

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:41.770
of the ruling party.

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:52.330
And yet, such is the prestige of the constitution
and recognition of the importance of the judiciary

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:59.140
and the rule of law that there has been no
effort, attempt, or even discussion of amending

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:05.640
the constitution to restore capital punishment
and abolish gay marriage.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:13.390
HAASS: Let me end, and then we'll open it
up, with a question I maybe should have begun

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:17.580
with, which is, give us a sense of why this
matters so much.

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:25.420
To what extent is what happens in South Africa
going to be strongly suggestive or influential

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:27.180
over what happens in the rest of Africa?

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:33.400
How much is South Africa simply sui generis
because of its own special history and also

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:37.300
because of its location?

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.180
CAMPBELL: It's influential.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.730
What is South Africa?

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:51.010
South Africa is an experiment in liberal parliamentary
democracy based on the protection of human

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:59.950
rights in a country and in a society that
was about as fractured as you could get, race,

00:22:59.950 --> 00:23:07.670
class, language, ethnic group, and yet it's
working.

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:11.640
This has an impact.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:20.240
It's difficult to establish exact cause and
effect, but I know from conversations that

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:32.020
I have had that what happened in South Africa
has played a role in the more positive trajectory

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:38.660
of countries like Nigeria where for the first
time the opposition came to power through

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:39.660
the ballot box.

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:43.160
HAASS: So this is, if you will, the soft power
of the South African example?

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:51.650
CAMPBELL: Yes, reinforced by the power of
South African economic penetration of other

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:52.650
parts of Africa.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:56.370
Cell phones, for example, in Nigeria are dominated
by South African companies.

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:59.440
HAASS: I want to ask one last question.

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:06.390
I've often thought that the person who doesn't
get enough of the credit for the last 22 years,

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:10.570
in particular for the peaceful transition
out of apartheid, is F.W.

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:11.570
de Klerk.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:12.570
CAMPBELL: Quite agree.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:13.570
HAASS: Oh, OK.

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:14.900
I thought it was going to be controversial.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:15.900
CAMPBELL: No, no.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:16.900
(Laughter.)

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:17.900
No.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:18.900
And he did win a Nobel Prize.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:19.900
HAASS: He did.

00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:22.440
But if you ask everyone, properly speaks about
Nelson Mandela.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:27.430
But one of the lessons I took from that and
what the Middle East often lacked in the Israeli/Palestinian

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:28.720
case was it takes two to tango.

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:29.720
CAMPBELL: It does.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:34.230
HAASS: And when you had someone like Yitzhak
Rabin or whatever, you never had potentially

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:37.720
somebody on the other side to be a partner
with.

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:38.800
It's a digression.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:44.130
I'm curious, what is de Klerk-what is today
the reputation in South Africa of Mandela

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.000
and de Klerk?

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:51.290
CAMPBELL: Well, Mandela, Mandela is a sort
of George Washington type.

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:55.830
De Klerk was present for Mandela's funeral.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:25:02.620
And whenever the camera sort of focused on
him, there were great big television screens

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:05.790
around the stadium, he was always applauded.

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:07.410
HAASS: Nice.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:14.470
CAMPBELL: So he is a person of respect.

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.530
He's not very active.

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:20.380
He essentially is retired.

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:29.020
Speaking of soft power, there's some evidence
that de Klerk's participation in an exchange

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:38.520
visit to the United States played a key role
in breaking him out of the Afrikaner apartheid

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.540
ghetto, if you like.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:44.460
HAASS: Interesting.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:48.430
As you can see, you've got one of this country's
or the world's leading experts on Africa.

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:52.660
So take advantage of the moment, raise your
hand, we'll get you a microphone.

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:58.570
Just let us know who you are, keep it short,
and we'll hear lots from Ambassador Campbell.

00:25:58.570 --> 00:26:00.030
Sure.

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:07.910
Q: My name is Larry Bridwell.

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:11.540
Is it working?

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:13.420
HAASS: It is.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:14.420
You have to project.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:15.420
CAMPBELL: Yeah, we can hear you.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:16.420
Q: OK.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:17.420
My name is Larry Bridwell and I teach international
business at PACE University and I have many

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:18.420
students from Africa.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.660
And I just got a term paper from a Nigerian
about the exchange rate and the management

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:24.050
of the exchange rate in Nigeria.

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:31.220
Could you comment on the current president
and your projection for the future of Nigeria?

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:35.000
HAASS: Now you're going to have to sell your
old book.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:36.000
(Laughter.)

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:38.310
I'm sorry we don't have that here, too.

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:41.980
We could have had a two-for-one sale or something
like that.

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:45.710
Like Amazon, if you like this book, you'll
like this one.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:56.570
CAMPBELL: Just very quickly, my view is that
President Buhari sees devaluing that naira,

00:26:56.570 --> 00:27:03.000
which is what the economists all want him
to do, as essentially putting an undue burden

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:11.340
on the Nigerian poor because so much of what
is consumed is imported.

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:18.090
And in fact, I don't think he has seen convincing
arguments to the contrary.

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:23.790
The arguments may be there, and Buhari may
indeed change his mind at some point in the

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.750
future, he's under a lot of pressure to do
so.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:34.251
I would suspect that more likely will be a
kind of dual exchange rate system, perhaps

00:27:34.251 --> 00:27:38.970
not unlike what existed under President Babangida.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:40.970
HAASS: OK.

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:43.980
Yes, ma'am, sure.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:45.980
Q: Working?

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:47.990
Yeah, hi.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.630
So you spoke about South Africa's-
HAASS: You have to introduce yourself.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:51.630
Q: Oh, Ruti Teitel.

00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:55.360
I'm a law professor and a member of the Council.

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:59.700
So you spoke about rule of law and South Africa's
leadership role, so I wondered if you could

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:03.800
comment on South Africa's role concerning
the International Criminal Court.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:07.531
Obviously, Africa was one of the continents
that was most supportive of the ICC.

00:28:07.531 --> 00:28:11.960
I haven't yet read your book, but I promise
to do that.

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:18.770
I wondered what your take was on South Africa's
recent messaging about the International Criminal

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:22.620
Court, its treatment of Bashir, et cetera.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:24.150
So I'm wondering if you could comment on that.

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:25.150
Yeah.

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:26.150
CAMPBELL: Absolutely schizoid, absolutely
schizoid.

00:28:26.150 --> 00:28:27.150
Q: Schizoid, I like that.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.370
HAASS: So for those of us who don't know,
can you give us 30 seconds of the basic facts

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:30.370
here?

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:31.370
CAMPBELL: Sure.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:34.430
The International Criminal Court is an institution
that was set up-

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:35.430
HAASS: I don't mean about that.

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:36.430
I meant about the South African position.

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:39.720
CAMPBELL: OK, the-
HAASS: You could do the ICC also.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:40.720
(Laughter.)

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:41.860
CAMPBELL: OK, OK.

00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:43.470
HAASS: Go ahead.

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:46.200
CAMPBELL: The Treaty of Rome that set up-(laughter)
-

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:47.920
HAASS: How about Grotius?

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:50.070
Let's go back to Grotius.

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:55.971
CAMPBELL: The Treaty of Rome that set up the
ICC has been incorporated into South African

00:28:55.971 --> 00:29:04.711
law so that when the Zuma administration failed
to arrest Bashir when he was in South Africa

00:29:04.711 --> 00:29:10.640
and in fact facilitated his departure, it
was violating South African law.

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:17.260
And there are suits in the South African court
system to that effect.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:24.500
So what you have got is you've got the Zuma
administration pursuing essentially one policy

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:28.390
which is not too different from Kenyatta's
in Kenya.

00:29:28.390 --> 00:29:38.890
But you've got the law and the institutions
which are essentially shaping another approach.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:43.390
And we'll have to see how the various law
cases work their way through.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:44.450
HAASS: OK.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:47.830
Yes, sir, right here at the first table.

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:50.330
Q: Thank you very much.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:54.420
My name is David Fararai (ph) and I'm from
the University of South Africa.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:30:03.170
I mean, just comparing Zimbabwe and South
Africa, don't you think that one of the reasons

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:08.990
why, you know, there's good governance in
South Africa relative to Zimbabwe is also

00:30:08.990 --> 00:30:18.670
the issue of, you know, rotational democracy
as well as active participation of the civil

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:21.760
society in the political system?

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:30.900
Because I think for democracy to thrive you
also need a very active civil society system

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:31.900
to thrive.

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:37.440
And in most cases, you and I might know that
there's been arrests when civil society tried

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:40.430
to raise the-
CAMPBELL: You're absolutely right.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:45.090
And further, South African civil society was
particularly strong because of its active

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.870
participation in the anti-apartheid struggle.

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:56.010
So I would argue South African civil society
is stronger than civil society in certain

00:30:56.010 --> 00:31:00.450
other countries that are usually regarded
as more mature democracies, such as Spain,

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:03.860
for exactly the reasons that you cited.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:07.520
Further, South African civil society isn't
new.

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:16.890
I mean, it is now some generations old, whereas
you quite rightly point out that is not the

00:31:16.890 --> 00:31:18.350
case in Zimbabwe.

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:23.230
Further, South Africa has regular, credible
elections.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:30.300
It's had four thus far, generally judged by
outside experts to be equal in quality to

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.490
those elections that take place in Japan or
the United States.

00:31:33.490 --> 00:31:36.070
Well, we all know what elections are like
in Zimbabwe.

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:38.170
HAASS: Let me just ask you a question before
we open it up.

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:42.860
There is a whole school of analysis which
says you have your prediction.

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:47.460
What would you have to see in order for you
to get nervous about that your optimism may

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:48.460
be misplaced?

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:52.420
Would it be, you mentioned, Mrs. Zuma being
elected?

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:53.420
CAMPBELL: No.

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:55.550
HAASS: What would basically shake you?

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:58.910
What would be the canary in the coalmine that
would really worry you?

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:06.210
CAMPBELL: What would really shake me would
be a decision made by the constitutional court,

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:11.980
and the last decisions have all been unanimous,
decision made by the constitutional court,

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:16.130
which is ignored by the administration.

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:22.650
Andrew Jackson, Mr. Madison has made his decision,
now let him enforce it, something like that,

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:23.680
that would shake me.

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:25.800
HAASS: OK, interesting.

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:27.210
Yes, ma'am?

00:32:27.210 --> 00:32:30.730
Please wait for a microphone.

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:31.730
We want to all hear you.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:32.820
Q: Mary Donovan.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.580
I teach world history.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:38.960
What about the universities in South Africa?

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:45.070
Are they now more open to students from the
lower classes, the poorer students?

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:47.350
Are more students going to the universities?

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:48.540
What's the situation?

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:51.710
CAMPBELL: Dramatically, dramatically better.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:53.970
All of the universities are integrated.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:33:05.440
The University of Cape Town, Wits, Stellenbosch
all have black majorities.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:14.650
Now, whites are disproportionately represented
at all of the high-quality universities and

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:17.950
the high-quality secondary schools.

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:24.810
But whites are 9 percent of the population
and blacks are 80 percent of the population.

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:28.300
So that process has gone very far.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:37.350
What has been a remarkable failure, though,
I think, has been primary education, particularly

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:41.430
in the townships and in the rural areas.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:49.900
And that that means is that too many black
students are not equipped to go into the modern

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:51.040
economy.

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:58.520
An example-if you telephone a stockbroker's
office in downtown Johannesburg, the person

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:01.950
who will answer the phone will likely be a
Zimbabwean.

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:08.490
She will be a Zimbabwean because in Zimbabwe
primary education did not collapse and it

00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:10.040
was all done in English.

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:16.569
Well, there are 11 legal languages in South
Africa and the South Africans themselves have

00:34:16.569 --> 00:34:22.760
reached no consensus as to in what language
should primary education be conducted.

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:24.620
HAASS: Yes, sir?

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:28.960
Q: Yes, some questions on income inequality.

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:31.440
HAASS: Steven, introduce yourself.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:32.690
Q: Sure.

00:34:32.690 --> 00:34:37.100
It's Steve Tananbaum, GoldenTree Asset Management.

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:43.760
A question about income inequality, and what's
been done for the last three to five years,

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:46.720
and why has it been unsuccessful?

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:52.850
And what do you think the prospects going
forward for income inequality over the intermediate,

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:55.609
call it, five to 10-year period is?

00:34:55.609 --> 00:35:00.770
And is this a big issue the way it is in some
of, you know, in, for instance, here where

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:02.109
it's clearly a big issue?

00:35:02.109 --> 00:35:03.190
CAMPBELL: Yeah.

00:35:03.190 --> 00:35:04.190
Yeah.

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:07.730
If you take a three- to five-year time frame,
I would suggest remarkably little has been

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:13.369
done to address questions of economic inequality.

00:35:13.369 --> 00:35:20.020
The signature programs are the so-called black
economic empowerment programs which I think

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:29.200
most observers would say have not been very
effective.

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:37.740
Essentially they revolve around goals that
are largely voluntary and have had the effect

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:43.440
of enriching a relatively small number of
people who are quite close to the ANC power

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:50.190
structure and have had remarkably little impact
on the mass of the population in the townships

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:55.020
and in the rural areas.

00:35:55.020 --> 00:36:08.930
Income distribution is so out of whack that
about 17 million South Africans receive some

00:36:08.930 --> 00:36:12.410
form of-
HAASS: What was the number?

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:14.119
CAMPBELL: Seventeen million out of-
HAASS: One-seven.

00:36:14.119 --> 00:36:17.030
CAMPBELL: -50 million receive some form of
assistance.

00:36:17.030 --> 00:36:25.250
They are mostly children, old people and widows,
17 million.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:31.520
There are only a little more than 3 million
who pay income tax.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:37.500
Now, a major source of government revenue
in South Africa is VAT.

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:44.920
So, I mean, poor people pay taxes, too, but
they don't pay income taxes.

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:54.990
And yet, that system of allowances, that sort
of social safety net only takes up between

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:59.740
3 and 4 percent of national income.

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:05.390
So from my perspective, this is clearly a
sort of ticking time bomb.

00:37:05.390 --> 00:37:09.930
HAASS: The young man next to you, Steve, right
there with the striped shirt.

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:13.511
Q: Hello, my name is Nelson E. Goodman (ph).

00:37:13.511 --> 00:37:19.210
I've worked with tech and social enterprises
in the U.S. and in developing/emerging markets.

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:26.020
So you made a point earlier about white migration
to South Africa, how it's increased actually

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:30.630
since the end of apartheid, and that made
me think about white political participation.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:35.950
Do you see an increase in rates of voting
or, you know, engagement in civil society

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:40.400
or broader political participation among these
whites that have returned?

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:42.000
I'd like you to go a little bit more into
that, please.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:44.440
HAASS: Was it migration or just natural population
increase?

00:37:44.440 --> 00:37:52.110
CAMPBELL: There is some white migration into
South Africa, but the percentage of the population

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:57.400
that is white is lower now than it was in
1994.

00:37:57.400 --> 00:38:00.040
In absolute numbers it's higher-
Q: OK.

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.680
CAMPBELL: -but not by very much.

00:38:03.680 --> 00:38:09.880
Political participation in South Africa continues
to largely be racially defined.

00:38:09.880 --> 00:38:17.950
In other words, whites tend to support and
participate in the Democratic Alliance; blacks

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:20.710
meet in the ANC.

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:26.220
An interesting thing will be to watch what
happens with the Democratic Alliance and whether

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:32.790
it can break out of the essentially white
and colored straitjacket, electoral straitjacket

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:34.340
that it's in.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:38.710
That's what we're all going to be watching
in terms of the local government elections

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:44.480
that are coming up in August, because unless
it can broaden its appeal to the majority

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:49.190
population, it in effect can never be the
party of government.

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:52.380
HAASS: Yes, ma'am?

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:55.570
Q: Thank you.

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:59.290
Jean Herskovits, State University of New York.

00:38:59.290 --> 00:39:04.160
John, I wonder if you could talk a little
bit about the relationship between South Africa

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:14.080
and its immediate neighbors now and evolving
in SADC and, beyond that, greater Africa,

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:20.340
because you mentioned the Zimbabwean answering
the phone, there are a lot of non-South African

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:28.350
Africans coming into South Africa, which creates
a political challenge there, as in many other

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:29.350
places.

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:33.190
So I'd like to hear your thoughts about that.

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:39.730
CAMPBELL: Perhaps best to approach it from
a number of different levels.

00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:46.330
Rather like here, South Africans tend to think
there are many more immigrants in the country

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:48.320
than there really are.

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:56.580
The percentage of the population in South
Africa that consists of either or combined

00:39:56.580 --> 00:40:01.460
legal immigrants and illegal immigrants is
about the same as the percentage here, it's

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:02.840
around 11 percent.

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:06.610
But South Africans think it's much higher
than that, and they will often talk about

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:12.550
this wave of people that have come across
the Zambezi River.

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:22.350
So I think we have a perceptions issue here,
part of it is particularly Nigerian and Congolese

00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:26.420
millionaires like to go to South Africa, they
build great, big, splashy houses and they're

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:32.130
very visible, and that, I think, adds to the
mix.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:37.470
In terms of a more formal relationship between
South Africa and its neighbors, under Zuma

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:40.180
I would say there's been a kind of recessional.

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:47.380
South Africa is not nearly as active as it
was under Mbeki on African issues, notwithstanding

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:57.490
the fact that South Africa campaigned very
hard to get Madam Zuma as the leader of the

00:40:57.490 --> 00:41:00.170
African Union.

00:41:00.170 --> 00:41:06.450
My own feeling is that unless or until there's
some sort of resolution of the situation in

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:14.600
Zimbabwe, SADC-Southern African Development
Corporation-will largely remain a dead letter.

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:18.800
HAASS: With the red jacket?

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:21.270
Q: Hi.

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:22.420
I'm Corsi (ph).

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:27.290
I'm South African and I work for a bank.

00:41:27.290 --> 00:41:31.990
Of course South Africa has its issues, as
do many other countries.

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:37.760
From your perspective-or at least I'd rather
try to focus on the more progressive aspect.

00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:41.900
So if we look at the fact that we have one
of the strongest constitutions-

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:42.900
CAMPBELL: Yes.

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:45.420
Q: -one of the most sophisticated financial
systems-

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:46.420
CAMPBELL: Yes.

00:41:46.420 --> 00:41:53.140
Q: -what would it take for South Africa to
get to the next level?

00:41:53.140 --> 00:42:05.270
CAMPBELL: I think the short answer to that
is jobs and jobs particularly for the unskilled.

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:12.830
If you can bring the unemployment rate down
from 50 percent in some of the townships,

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:19.020
you are going to greatly bring down the levels
of poverty and you're going to increase the

00:42:19.020 --> 00:42:23.140
amount of consumption that that becomes possible.

00:42:23.140 --> 00:42:24.140
HAASS: Can I just interrupt?

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:25.140
I don't understand.

00:42:25.140 --> 00:42:28.900
When you say jobs for the unskilled, do you
mean jobs that match a lack of skill?

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:29.900
CAMPBELL: Yes.

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:32.670
HAASS: Or do you mean bringing the unskilled
up so they can take more sophisticated jobs?

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:37.270
CAMPBELL: Well, you do both, of course, but
the latter takes much longer, whereas you

00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:44.270
could, through changes in labor policy, you
could bring in or you could create unskilled

00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:45.480
jobs fairly quickly.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.820
HAASS: Like infrastructure or something?

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:49.220
CAMPBELL: Yeah, sure.

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:52.580
HAASS: Anything else that you-I mean, the
question about what would it take for South

00:42:52.580 --> 00:42:58.580
Africa, say, to increase per capita GDP significantly
or whatever.

00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:01.109
What else would you recommend if they hire
you as the adviser?

00:43:01.109 --> 00:43:06.880
CAMPBELL: If they hire me as the adviser,
the first thing I would try to do is a campaign

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:11.540
to get South Africans to invest the capital
that they've got stuffed under their mattress

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:13.470
in their own economy.

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:18.100
I mean, South Africa right now is great on
exporting capital to other parts of Africa

00:43:18.100 --> 00:43:19.790
as opposed to investing it at home.

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:24.830
If you can invest that capital at home and
if you can create lots and lots of unskilled

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:31.210
jobs, bring the unemployment rate down, you
then, I think, see the country moving economically

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:33.570
to a higher level.

00:43:33.570 --> 00:43:37.900
HAASS: Yes, ma'am?

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:40.350
Q: My name is Bhakti Mirchandani.

00:43:40.350 --> 00:43:43.090
I work for a hedge fund, and I'm a term member.

00:43:43.090 --> 00:43:44.430
Thank you for your comments.

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:50.140
We've spoken in this discussion about widening
the wealth gap between blacks and whites,

00:43:50.140 --> 00:43:51.790
increasing integration of universities.

00:43:51.790 --> 00:43:57.290
But one substantial issue, from my perspective,
in South Africa is the abysmal graduation

00:43:57.290 --> 00:44:01.910
rates, the very high dropout rates out of
higher education and also the relative unaffordability,

00:44:01.910 --> 00:44:04.740
even for students getting scholarships relative
to the average income.

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:06.380
What's your take on solving that?

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:07.520
CAMPBELL: All of that's true.

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:09.320
All of that's absolutely true.

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:14.590
I tend to think that for a country at the
particular stage of development that South

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:21.420
Africa is at that you need to start with primary
education as opposed to starting it at the

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:24.590
university level.

00:44:24.590 --> 00:44:26.320
There's an irony here.

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:34.900
Some of South Africa's universities, Wits,
Cape Town, Stellenbosch, Durban, are some

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:37.140
of the best in the world.

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:42.550
And yet, there are other universities, in
many cases founded under apartheid and intended

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:49.960
essentially for black students and grotesquely
underfunded, which are much more similar to

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:51.990
universities you find in other parts of Africa.

00:44:51.990 --> 00:44:53.880
OK, big problem.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:59.060
To raise those up is going to require a lot
of resources.

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:03.980
Same is true with doing something about primary
education.

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:07.310
Right now, I think it would be better to spend
the money on primary education.

00:45:07.310 --> 00:45:10.790
HAASS: Mr. Shriver?

00:45:10.790 --> 00:45:14.830
Q: I'm Donald Shriver from Union Theological
Seminary.

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:23.390
I'm a graduate, so to speak, of the divestment
movement of the 1980s, and I would prefer

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:25.690
investment rather than divestment.

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:30.800
I'm wondering, especially for those of us
who have a sizable part of our own retirement

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:40.470
income from corporate profits and so forth,
what areas of investment do you think would

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:44.810
be most beneficial to contemporary South Africans?

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:51.610
I ask it partly so that if we have any vote
in a stock meeting and so forth we at least

00:45:51.610 --> 00:46:00.130
need the courage, invest in projects that
will do some good, especially for that employment

00:46:00.130 --> 00:46:02.760
challenge in South Africa.

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:10.300
CAMPBELL: Anything that generates jobs for
unskilled people.

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:14.119
Let me give you a specific example-call centers.

00:46:14.119 --> 00:46:22.470
Now, we all know that there are call centers
in India, it's a huge industry.

00:46:22.470 --> 00:46:28.980
And the industry is so highly developed that
in fact those that answer the phone are even

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:31.330
taught to speak with American accents.

00:46:31.330 --> 00:46:35.270
Well, why not a call center in Soweto?

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:41.300
You know, I mean, we're not talking about
something that should be that difficult to

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:42.300
do.

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:46.010
And yet, so far as I am aware, there are no
call centers in South Africa.

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:47.450
HAASS: Interesting.

00:46:47.450 --> 00:46:49.720
OK, one last question.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:50.740
Sir?

00:46:50.740 --> 00:47:00.130
Q: Tom Walsh with UPF, an interfaith organization,
and I participate in the CFR's Religion and

00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:01.640
Foreign Policy Workshop.

00:47:01.640 --> 00:47:10.370
But I'd like to ask you to comment on the
religious landscape and maybe draw an analogy

00:47:10.370 --> 00:47:18.460
or comparison with Nigeria where there's been
very serious problems, Christianity and Islam.

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:25.660
Has in general religion, faith-based organizations,
et cetera, been a significant plus factor?

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:31.650
And to what extent is that a strong factor
in the South African exceptionalism, let's

00:47:31.650 --> 00:47:32.920
say, within Africa?

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:35.140
CAMPBELL: It's enormously important.

00:47:35.140 --> 00:47:45.890
Albertina Sisulu, a major figure in the liberation
movement, she was told one time that Afrikaners

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:49.700
were really quite frightened about what the
future would look like.

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:51.870
And she responded, well, what are they afraid
of?

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:54.400
We're all Christians.

00:47:54.400 --> 00:48:02.460
In other words, the settlement in 1994 didn't
just drop out of the sky.

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:12.970
And the churches played an enormously important
role in preparing the way forward.

00:48:12.970 --> 00:48:18.380
I would argue they were far more important
than the South African communist party or

00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:21.180
the whole Marxist/Leninist way of thinking.

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:23.440
But your question goes further.

00:48:23.440 --> 00:48:29.220
By the way, there is a small Islamic minority
in South Africa, relations are fine, it's

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:30.300
not an issue.

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:35.859
Your question goes further, though, because
there has been an explosive growth of Pentecostalism

00:48:35.859 --> 00:48:47.859
in South Africa that would appear to be changing
the way people relate to the political process,

00:48:47.859 --> 00:48:49.530
changing it for the better.

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:56.580
It's interesting that Jacob Zuma no less and
the leader of the Democratic Alliance are

00:48:56.580 --> 00:48:58.840
both Pentecostal preachers.

00:48:58.840 --> 00:49:01.710
HAASS: We've come to the appointed hour.

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:04.460
I want to thank John.

00:49:04.460 --> 00:49:14.230
And again, he really is one of the true experts,
a real voice of knowledge and reason when

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:15.980
it comes to all things African.

00:49:15.980 --> 00:49:20.281
It's interesting that his two books, first
on Nigeria and now South Africa, cover what

00:49:20.281 --> 00:49:28.770
many of us think are the two most influential
and important countries south of the Sahara.

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:31.840
The book is called "Morning in South Africa."

00:49:31.840 --> 00:49:36.400
It's less than 200 pages, so you can read
it in a morning anywhere.

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:37.400
(Laughter.)

00:49:37.400 --> 00:49:43.260
And you can buy it in a minute here, so I
urge you to do it.

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:48.130
There's food and drink here, lots of opportunities
to ask John all the questions that we didn't

00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:49.630
have time for.

00:49:49.630 --> 00:49:51.900
And, Mr. Ambassador, thank you and congratulations.

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:57.480
CAMPBELL: Thank you very much.

00:49:57.480 --> 00:50:00.060
(Applause.)

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:02.650
(END)

